+# weewx-sqm-le
+
+WeeWX SQM-LE (Ethernet) support via a custom WeeWX service. This extension polls the SQM-LE over TCP, injects
+current readings into loop packets, and archives median values each interval. It computes derived sky metrics
+(NELM, NSU, cd/m²) and optional solar/lunar context (solar altitude, lunar altitude, lunar phase) using Skyfield.
+
+## What this provides
+
+- TCP polling of an SQM-LE using `rx`
+- Clean parsing of SQM responses
+- Derived metrics: `cdm2`, `nsu`, `nelm`
+- Loop packet augmentation
+- Median aggregation at archive time
+- Daytime/invalid filtering (`sqm <= 0.0` is discarded)
+
+## Quick Start
+
+1. Install this package into the same Python environment as WeeWX.
+2. Add the service to `weewx.conf`.
+3. Ensure archive schema includes the SQM fields.
+
+## Example `weewx.conf` snippet
+
+```
+[SQM-LE]
+    host = 192.168.1.119   # SQM-LE IP address or hostname
+    port = 10001           # SQM-LE TCP port (default 10001)
+    poll_interval = 10     # seconds between polls (10 = 10 seconds, 600 = 10 minutes, 900 = 15 minutes)
+    socket_timeout = 2.0   # seconds to wait for SQM-LE response
+    max_samples = 300      # max samples kept for median aggregation
+    ephemeris = de421.bsp  # Skyfield ephemeris file
+    skyfield_cache = /var/lib/weewx/skyfield  # Skyfield cache directory
+    solar_alt_max = -18.0  # skip polling when sun is above this altitude (deg)
+
+[Engine]
+    [[Services]]
+        data_services = user.sqmle.Service  # add SQM-LE to data_services only
+```
+
+Note: WeeWX writes archive records on the `archive_interval` (often 300 seconds / 5 minutes).
+SQM values are stored only when an archive record is written, even if you poll more frequently.
+Do not remove other service groups (prep/process/archive/report); only add SQM-LE to `data_services`.
+If you want SQM values in `daily_captures`, reinstall the extension so the installer can patch the Conditions skin templates.
+
+## Archive Fields
+
+Add fields to your archive schema if they are not present:
+
+- `sqm`
+- `sqmTemp`
+- `sqmTime`
+- `nelm`
+- `cdm2`
+- `nsu`
+- `solarAlt`
+- `lunarAlt`
+- `lunarPhase`
+
+## Notes
+
+- This package does not bundle WeeWX; run it inside a WeeWX environment.
+- The service is designed to avoid blocking the WeeWX engine.
+- If `skyfield` is installed and station latitude/longitude are available, lunar and solar fields are computed.
+- If `solar_alt_max` is set, polling is skipped whenever the sun is above that altitude.
+- `max_sample_age` controls how long the latest SQM sample is considered fresh for loop packets (defaults to `poll_interval * 3`). Older samples are not emitted in current JSON, but the last `sqmTime` still reports when the last reading occurred.
+- Suggested thresholds for `solar_alt_max`:
+  - `-6.0` for civil twilight
+  - `-12.0` for nautical twilight
+  - `-18.0` for astronomical night (recommended for dark-sky measurements)
+  - Example: `solar_alt_max = -18.0` starts polling when the sun drops below -18° after sunset and stops polling when the sun rises above -18° before sunrise.
+
+## Compatibility
+
+- Tested with WeeWX 5.2.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- Install the `weewx-conditions` extension:
+
+```text
+https://github.com/open-astro/weewx-conditions
+```
+
+- Install `skyfield` (for lunar altitude/phase calculations):
+
+```bash
+sudo -H python3 -m pip install skyfield
+```
+
+## Installation (Linux)
+
+These steps assume WeeWX is already installed and running.
+
+### Option A: WeeWX extension (recommended)
+
+1. Build the extension tarball:
+
+```bash
+cd /path/to/weewx-sqm-le
+python3 build_package.py --set-version 0.1.1
+```
+
+2. Install it with `weectl`:
+
+```bash
+sudo weectl extension install weewx-sqm-le_0.1.1.tar.gz
+```
+
+3. The installer will test the SQM-LE connection and Skyfield; install aborts on failure.
+4. The installer will also patch the `weewx-conditions` skin templates under `/etc/weewx/skins/Conditions` to add SQM fields.
+5. Restart WeeWX.
+
+### Option B: pip install
+
+1. Install into the same Python environment that WeeWX uses.
+
+Example using system Python:
+
+```bash
+cd /path/to/weewx-sqm-le
+python3 -m pip install .
+```
+
+If WeeWX runs in a virtualenv, activate it first and then run the same `pip install .`.
+
+2. Update `weewx.conf` with the `SQM-LE` section and service entry (see below).
+3. Restart the WeeWX service.
+
+## Quick SQM-LE Connectivity Test
+
+Before installing, you can verify the SQM-LE responds on the network (update IP/port):
+
+```bash
+python3 - <<'PY'
+import socket
+s=socket.create_connection(("192.168.1.119",10001),timeout=2)
+s.sendall(b"rx")
+print(s.recv(1024).decode("utf-8","replace"))
+s.close()
+PY
+```
+
+After installing you can verify the DB was updated correclty
+
+```bash
+sqlite3 /var/lib/weewx/weewx.sdb \
+"PRAGMA table_info(archive);"
+```
+
+It should show the following
+
+```bash
+115|sqm|REAL|0||0
+116|sqmTemp|REAL|0||0
+117|nelm|REAL|0||0
+118|cdm2|REAL|0||0
+119|nsu|REAL|0||0
+120|solarAlt|REAL|0||0
+121|lunarAlt|REAL|0||0
+122|lunarPhase|REAL|0||0
+```
+
+## Uninstalling
+
+```bash
+weectl extension uninstall weewx-sqm-le
+```
+
+Note: Uninstall does not remove archive database columns added for SQM.
